The [Unreleased] section had accumulated eight `### Added`, six `### Fixed`,
two `### Documentation` and one `### Changed`, interleaved -- so a reader
scanning for what changed had to know that "Added" appears eight separate times
and that the eighth is not a continuation of the seventh. The changelog-audit
playbook's Phase 2 names exactly this ("merge duplicates, harmonize
granularity"), and today's four entries made it worse.

MECHANICAL AND LOSSLESS BY CONSTRUCTION. Bullets are regrouped under one
heading per category, order preserved within each category. The edit asserts
its own preservation before writing: every top-level bullet must survive as a
multiset, and no non-heading content line may be added or dropped. 386 bullets
in, 386 out; 386 content lines in, 386 out.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. The playbook also invites trimming detail
toward a birds'-eye view. 386 bullets across 283k characters is a real
calibration question and it is a judgment call about other people's entries, so
it is left alone -- this change is only the structural half, which is
unambiguous.

INCIDENTALLY THE FIELD TEST FOR INV-zamoh. That item's closure recorded one
thing not yet observed: no real docs-only branch had gone through auto-pr since
the fix, because #599 itself touched code. This branch touches CHANGELOG.md and
nothing else, so `smart-test --manifest` writes exactly the manifest that used
to kill the push --

    # Reason: no test-relevant files changed (docs/config only)
    # Selected tests: 0

-- and the run that carries this commit is that observation.

